toricUniversalMarkov -- the universal Markov basis
**************************************************

  * Usage: 
        U = toricUniversalMarkov A
        I = toricUniversalMarkov(A, R)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, the configuration matrix
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, with one generator for each
        column of $A$
  * Outputs:
      * U, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, the universal Markov basis of
        $A$
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, an ideal in $R$ generated by
        the universal Markov basis of $A$

Description
===========

The universal Markov basis, often denoted $U(A)$, of a configuration matrix $A$
is the union of all minimal Markov bases of $A$. This method computes the
universal Markov basis of $A$ and returns the elements as the rows of a matrix.
Similarly to *note markovBases: markovBases,, if a ring $R$ is supplied, then
the result is an ideal generated by the universal Markov basis.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : A = matrix "7,8,9,10";                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|              1       4                                                      |
|o1 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : toricUniversalMarkov A                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = | -1 2  -1 0  |                                                         |
|     | -1 1  1  -1 |                                                         |
|     | 0  -1 2  -1 |                                                         |
|     | 4  0  -2 -1 |                                                         |
|     | 4  -1 0  -2 |                                                         |
|     | 3  1  -1 -2 |                                                         |
|     | 3  0  1  -3 |                                                         |
|     | 2  2  0  -3 |                                                         |
|                                                                             |
|              8       4                                                      |
|o2 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : toricUniversalMarkov(A, QQ[x_1 .. x_4])                                 |
|                                                                             |
|             2                       2          4    2     4      2   3      |
|o3 = ideal (x  - x x , x x  - x x , x  - x x , x  - x x , x  - x x , x x  -  |
|             2    1 3   2 3    1 4   3    2 4   1    3 4   1    2 4   1 2    |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        2   3      3   2 2    3                                              |
|     x x , x x  - x , x x  - x )                                             |
|      3 4   1 3    4   1 2    4                                              |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : Ideal of QQ[x ..x ]                                                     |
|                  1   4                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The function computes the universal Markov basis elements using the the fiber
graph of $A$; see *note fiberGraph: fiberGraph,. An irreducible binomial $x^u -
x^v$ is an element of the universal Markov basis if and only if $u$ and $v$
belong to different connected components of the fiber graph.



See also
========

  * *note markovBases: markovBases, -- all minimal Markov bases of a
    configuration matrix
  * *note fiberGraph: fiberGraph, -- generating fibers of a configuration
    matrix

Ways to use toricUniversalMarkov:
=================================

  * "toricUniversalMarkov(Matrix)"
  * "toricUniversalMarkov(Matrix,Ring)"

